The three sounds beside this file - thud.wav, hum.wav and click.wav - are not
recordings of anything and are nobody's work but this project's. They are
arithmetic, and they carry the same terms the rest of the repository does: MIT,
Copyright (c) 2026 whisky, see LICENSE at the root.

They are written down here for the reason the normal maps in
assets/textures/construct/ are: nothing downstream can tell a generated sound
from a recorded one, so if it is not said here it is not said anywhere.

Where these descriptions came from, honestly. This file was supposed to go in
with the sounds on 2026-08-31 and did not - it was named in the audit, the
handover said to add the directory, and only the .wav files were staged. The
generator was never in the repository either, by the same rule that keeps the
Blender scripts out. So the original recipe is gone, and what follows was
**measured back out of the files themselves** on 2026-09-03 rather than copied
from anything. It describes what they are; it is not what was typed to make
them, and a regenerated set will not be sample-identical.

All three are mono, sixteen-bit, 44100 Hz. Nothing in the engine cares about
the rate - a .csnd keeps whatever its source had, and the mixer resamples - so
these are 44100 only because that is what they were made at.

  thud.wav    0.3000 s, 13230 frames, peaks at 0.85

      A downward pitch sweep under an exponential decay: the tone falls from
      about 117 Hz at the start to about 33 Hz at the end, and the amplitude
      falls by roughly a factor of e^4 over the length, which leaves under two
      percent of the peak at the end. That is a struck thump rather than a
      note. Its reference distance in the sandbox was measured rather than
      chosen and is six units - everything that lands is ten to twenty away.

  hum.wav     0.5000 s, 22050 frames, peaks at 0.55

      Four partials held flat for the whole length, with no envelope at all:

          60 Hz   relative strength 1.00
          90 Hz                     0.45
         180 Hz                     0.18
         270 Hz                     0.07

      **Every one of the four is a whole number of cycles in half a second**,
      which is the whole reason this loops seamlessly: 30, 45, 90 and 135
      cycles. Measured across the join, the step is 312 against a largest
      ordinary adjacent step of 313 - an ordinary step rather than a
      discontinuity. Change any of those four frequencies to something that is
      not a multiple of two hertz and the loop will click, once per half
      second, forever.

  click.wav   0.0300 s, 1323 frames, peaks at 0.70

      Broadband noise under a fast exponential decay - no partial anywhere in
      the spectrum stands more than about two percent above its neighbors, and
      the zero crossings sit around eleven kilohertz throughout, which is what
      noise measures as rather than what any tone does. The amplitude is down
      to a thousandth of the peak by the end.

What to do if these are ever regenerated: keep the hum's four frequencies on
whole cycles, keep the file lengths, and re-measure this file afterwards rather
than trusting it.
